ifa/160 -- a list read out of a dict and compared to an empty literal. `p == []` calls list.__eq__, which does `if l[i] != self[i]` (__pyc__.py:1400); `l` is the empty literal, whose CreationSet has arity 0, no positional slots and nothing ever written to its element channel, so `l[i]` is bottom and `!=` on it is unresolved. At run time the read never happens -- the length guard `if lself != ll: return False` returned first -- so this is unreachable code being type-checked, not a real union. CPython prints False. shedskin compiles it: its list type has no arity, so both literals are `list<T>` and indexing an empty one is well-typed. Flips to PASS when an index into an arity-0 contour is treated as the dead code it is. Two attempts are recorded in ifa/160 as measured-dead: suppressing the violations (they are re-raised in other contours) and letting arity-0 containers join (it merges into an arbitrary contour, leaving the element bottom).
